# Security

This is an application skeleton. Configure a private reporting channel and a
support policy before publication; never post credentials in public issues.

## Local transport defaults

HTTP and gRPC require literal loopback listen IPs and use plaintext. HTTP exposes
only unauthenticated health checks until application assembly registers routes.
gRPC requires a bearer token with at least 32 printable non-whitespace ASCII
characters. Never pass tokens in URLs or log them. Unary handlers have bounded
messages, deadlines and panic recovery; application failures need explicit safe
mapping with infrastructure/rpc.Error. New streaming RPCs need their own lifetime
and message policies. Before external deployment add TLS, caller identity,
per-operation authorization, request limits and an explicit listener policy.

Adding HTTP mapping does not install authorization automatically. Review every
new route and adapter. Protobuf schemas do not replace domain validation. There
is no database, migration or business access-control policy by default.

## Optional agents

Only trusted startup configuration selects model endpoints; API keys are sent
there. Redirects are disabled and URL credentials, queries and fragments rejected.
No network endpoint, history store or business tool is exposed automatically.
Use one shared runner for its per-instance capacity limit. Authorize and validate
all tools independently of model instructions. Deadline and token limits bound
cooperative execution; tools must honor context cancellation. Local model fixtures
do not establish real-provider or production security acceptance.

CLI contract build/check commands execute pinned Go plugins and Buf, consuming trusted application
source and configuration. They are not a sandbox for untrusted tools or source.
The application contains no generator source. Contract operations share a temporary
.nostosx.lock with CLI additions; never remove an active owner's lock. Plugin
failure preserves published output; normal publication failures attempt rollback.
Process termination can leave a lock or staging directory. Review generated changes
before building or deploying them.
